8. NOTICE OF INFRINGEMENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

8.1  If you believe that material associated with BeeLink infringes your
     rights, send a notice to the copyright owner through
     https://github.com/ItsNobodyX containing:

     (a) identification of the work you claim is infringed;
     (b) the location of the allegedly infringing material;
     (c) your name, address and contact details;
     (d) a statement of your good-faith belief that the use is unauthorised;
     (e) a statement that the information is accurate and that you are the
         rights holder or authorised to act for them;
     (f) your physical or electronic signature.

8.2  Notices meeting the requirements of 17 U.S.C. 512(c)(3) will be handled
     under the notice-and-takedown procedure of the Digital Millennium
     Copyright Act. Equivalent notices under the Information Technology Act,
     2000 and the Information Technology (Intermediary Guidelines and Digital
     Media Ethics Code) Rules, 2021 (India), and under Articles 14 to 17 of
     Directive 2000/31/EC and the Digital Services Act, Regulation (EU)
     2022/2065, will be handled in the same manner.

8.3  Counter-notices and misrepresentation claims will be assessed on the same
     basis. Knowingly filing a false notice may itself give rise to liability.

9. INTERNATIONAL PROTECTION AND GOVERNING STATUTES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

9.1  BeeLink is protected in every state party to the Berne Convention for the
     Protection of Literary and Artistic Works, the WIPO Copyright Treaty
     (1996) and the Agreement on Trade-Related Aspects of Intellectual
     Property Rights (TRIPS). No registration is required for that protection
     to subsist.

9.2  INDIA. Protected under the Copyright Act, 1957 (Act 14 of 1957), as
     amended, including section 13 (subsistence of copyright), section 14
     (exclusive rights), section 51 (infringement), section 57 (author's
     special rights) and section 63 (offences). The BeeLink name and mark are
     protected under the Trade Marks Act, 1999. Electronic distribution and
     intermediaries are addressed by the Information Technology Act, 2000 and
     the rules made under it. Civil remedies include injunction, damages,
     account of profits, delivery up and seizure; section 63 provides for
     imprisonment of not less than six months, extendable to three years, and
     a fine of not less than fifty thousand rupees, extendable to two lakh
     rupees.

9.3  UNITED STATES. Protected under Title 17 of the United States Code,
     including sections 106 (exclusive rights), 501 (infringement), 504
     (damages, including statutory damages of up to 150,000 US dollars per
     work for wilful infringement), 512 (notice and takedown) and 1202
     (integrity of copyright management information). The BeeLink name and mark
     are protected under the Lanham Act, 15 U.S.C. 1051 et seq., including
     section 43(a) on false designation of origin.

9.4  EUROPEAN UNION AND EEA. Protected under Directive 2001/29/EC on the
     harmonisation of copyright and related rights, Directive (EU) 2019/790 on
     copyright in the digital single market, and the implementing legislation
     of each member state, together with the case law of the Court of Justice
     of the European Union. The BeeLink name and mark are protected under
     Directive (EU) 2015/2436 and Regulation (EU) 2017/1001, and through the
     European Union Intellectual Property Office.

9.5  UNITED KINGDOM. Protected under the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act
     1988, including the moral rights provisions in Chapter IV, and under the
     Trade Marks Act 1994.

9.6  OTHER JURISDICTIONS. Protection is claimed under the corresponding
     copyright, trade mark, unfair competition and database laws of every other
     country in which BeeLink is made available, in each case to the fullest
     extent permitted by that law.

9.7  Nothing in this notice excludes any right or remedy available under the
     mandatory law of the country in which a user resides.

